{"timestamp": 1778519435.3065434, "duration_sec": 2.2793984413146973, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[3, "Well, I got them all, you know, I got them all pointed out. I got a report on every single one of them. Which one would you want me to start with? I got\u2014 my competition's everywhere. Everybody's my competition."], [1, "There are dozens of street promoters like Alberto, and because there are so many of them, all working on commission, Alberto's success depends on his charisma. He has to give the best performance. And so Alberto has his own strategies to try to stand out and make a good impression. Besides workshopping comedic material on the job, he told me this one other thing. He tends to play up how American he is. It's not a hard thing for him to do because like many people who grew up along the border, Alberto traveled back and forth a lot. He lived in Arizona but crossed the border weekly. His English is good. He describes himself as being \"from the border.\"."], [3, "So I grew up, you know, going to school and then coming down the weekends down to Mexico. And a lot of kids grew up like that. Because there's a lot of people in Agua Dulce that were\u2014 oh, I forgot to mention I was deported."], [1, "Alberto was deported to Mexico in 2010. In fact, I learned that almost all of Molar City's street promoters are deportees. It is in fact their Americanness, their American English, that makes them ideal candidates for this role."], [3, "It helped me break the ice. It helps me bond with people. Right away they can tell that I lived in America."], [1, "Oh, they bring it up?"], [3, "Yeah, they'll say it like that. They'll be like, \"Oh, I can hear you. Oh yeah, you lived in the States, huh?\" I'm like, \"Yes. Got me a little agent McCasian over there.\" So it makes them\u2014 makes me relate to them more and feel more relaxed."], [1, "Alberto tells me he'll recognize familiar faces crossing into Mexico from time to time. So-and-so from high school, his friend's uncle. But he doesn't get to see his family so often. They all live in Arizona and don't like to come visit."], [3, "They really\u2014 they really only come when they have to."], [1, "Yeah."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["immigration", "deportation", "border life", "mexico", "united states", "street marketing", "sales commission", "charisma", "identity", "american culture", "bilingual language", "cross border travel", "family separation", "social adaptation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778519498.1525314, "duration_sec": 2.137331008911133, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Good morning, it's February 18th and this is your daily brief in AI. Here's everything you need to know."], [1, "AI takes center stage at BioAsia 2026 in Hyderabad as Asia's leading life sciences and health tech forum opens its 23rd edition with a sharp focus on AI, automation, and data science to accelerate healthcare innovation. More than 175 exhibitors spanning pharmaceuticals, biotechnology, medtech, digital health, and advanced manufacturing showcase a broad tech-bio ecosystem. The event brings researchers, entrepreneurs, investors, and policymakers together to push the tech-bio revolution and address global health challenges. Telangana highlights its strong policies, skilled workforce, and premier educational institutions as key drivers of growth in life sciences. The opening ceremony, led by the State Chief Minister, included the Genome Valley Excellence Award for Cancer Gene Therapy to Professor Bruce L. Levine. Inauguration and awards underscore Telangana's leadership in life sciences and recognize Levine's work in cancer gene therapy. Telangana also unveiled initiatives such as the Young India Skills University and the Telangana School of Life Sciences to build talent in mRNA technology, gene therapy, and AI. Recent moves cited include the Telangana Next Gen Life Sciences Policy, Genome Valley expansion, the launch of OneBio and multiple global capability centers in Hyderabad, The event emphasized ongoing collaborations among research institutions, corporates, startups, MSMEs, and government to create a trusted, future-ready life sciences ecosystem in Telangana. Telangana Rising 2047 envisions making the state a $1 trillion economy by year 2034 and $3 trillion by year 2047 through cross-sector collaboration across research labs, industry, startups, MSMEs, and government. The Telangana Life Sciences Policy 2026-30 promotes cross-border collaboration, international capital, and translation of breakthroughs into healthcare solutions to position Telangana as a global life sciences hub. Plans include shifting from supply-led manufacturing to solution-driven leadership by building talent in mRNA, gene editing, and AI-driven drug discovery, while boosting Hyderabad's role in medical tourism."], [2, "Unilever is partnering with a major cloud provider to run an AI-first digital overhaul, migrating its data and cloud platform to Google Cloud to speed demand generation and deliver actionable insights. The initiative centers on flagship brands Dove, Vaseline, and Hellmann's as consumer journeys become more AI-driven and conversational, aiming to redefine how consumer packaged goods are discovered and shopped. Leaders describe it as a milestone in modernizing legacy systems and boosting agility and consumer engagement in the CPG sector. Market reaction showed UL trading near its 52-week high, with a pre-market around $74.65 and approaching the annual high of $74.97. Unilever reported strong fiscal 2025 results, with total sales up 3.8% to \u20ac50.5 billion across Beauty Wellbeing, personal care, and home care. The collaboration is framed as part of a broader shift toward agentic consumer journeys where AI surfaces influence product discovery and selection. It aims to redefine how brands are found and purchased, aligning with increasingly conversational journeys guided by intelligent digital agents. There is mention of repurposing a former Unilever soap factory in Wales into a modular data center site, signaling broader data infrastructure development alongside the cloud migration. The initiative seeks more personalized consumer engagement and more efficient marketing investments, signaling a move toward AI-driven experiences in the CPG space. Unilever's longstanding relationship with Accenture, renewed in 2024 to scale GenAI, complements Horizon 3 Labs' efforts in Toronto that reportedly runs hundreds of AI applications. The partnership signals a broader industry shift toward AI-enabled transformation in marketing, data infrastructure, and automated business processes within consumer goods, with overhauls of marketing processes to establish new measurement standards, increase conversion rates, and accelerate AI adoption across global."], [3, "Operations."], [4, "The ASM AI Fest 2026 runs through February 28th, showcasing an AI-first mindset across teaching, research, learning methods, and administration to prepare graduates and staff for pervasive AI adoption. The festival features world-class AI workshops that move beyond awareness to practical AI applications, aiming for AI to amplify human capability rather than substitute it. It concludes with a closing ceremony on February 28th, reinforcing ASM's leadership in AI-integrated education across MBA, PGDM, MCA, engineering, undergraduate studies, and school and junior college. A press release includes a photo and logo link and carries an advertorial disclaimer noting the content was provided by PR Newswire and not authored by Business Standard journalists. The initiative is described as one of the region's largest AI-driven academic efforts and positions ASM as a pioneer in AI-integrated education aligned with national AI objectives. As part of the sustained transformation, ASM will form institute-wide AI task forces, establish the ASM AI Center of Excellence, deploy the Navdrishti app for automated student lifecycle management, and introduce AI into PGDM dual specialization programs. The long-term roadmap includes institute-wise AI task forces, a center of excellence for training and research, and the Navdrishti app with AI-enabled placements and broader expansion into academics and finances. Institutional AI roadmap highlights creating task forces, a COE for training and research, and Navdrishti for automated student lifecycle management, with initial AI-enabled placements and later expansion. AI integration spans the entire ASM academic spectrum, from MBA, PGEM, MCA, and engineering to undergraduate and school junior college, preparing learners for a future with pervasive AI adoption. A PGDM dual specialization program with AI integration was launched, signaling program-level AI incorporation across curricula. Program implications include AI-driven analytics for MBA PGDM, ML tools for MCA engineering, AI-influenced entrepreneurship, and data-driven decisions for undergraduates, and foundational AI literacy for younger students. The initiative aligns with India's India AI Mission and Digital India, aiming to position India as a global hub for AI talent and to embed AI across education and administration."], [3, "Ant Group expands its open-source AI family with two trillion-parameter models, Ling-2.5-1T and Ring-2.5-1T, released under open licenses on Hugging Face and ModelScope. The move continues Ling's ecosystem expansion following Ling 2.0 unveiled in late 2025 and positions Ant Group as a key proponent of open AI distribution for researchers and developers. Ling-2.5-1T is described as a flagship general language model focused on efficient reasoning and agent interaction, capable of handling context lengths up to 1 million tokens while using about 5,890 tokens to reach frontier-like results. Ring-2.5-1T is a reasoning-optimized model with a hybrid linear architecture, delivering strong mathematical performance on IMO 2025 and CMO 2025 benchmarks. Both models are part of a coordinated Ling, Ring, and Ming strategy aimed at enterprise AI deployments that combine language understanding, reasoning, and multimodal capabilities for financial services. The open-release approach seeks to accelerate ecosystem adoption by enabling external developers to build domain-specific finance applications, potentially reducing costs and enabling larger-scale deployments. Ant Group frames these releases within a broader fintech trend of integrating large language models and reasoning systems into tasks such as compliance, customer interactions, and risk assessment. Open Access Positions: Ant Group among global contributors to open large-scale AI and supports adapting models for financial domain tasks by external developers. The Ling-2.5-1T release emphasizes efficient reasoning and agent interaction, while Ring-2.5-1T targets higher-order mathematical reasoning and benchmark performance. Overall, the announcements underscore a move toward open, collaborative AI infrastructure designed to support enterprise-grade finance use."], [0, "Cases. Tech is expanding its AI-driven frontline performance ecosystem, anchored by Realskill and Perform, to serve more than 100 enterprise clients and over 25,000 frontline agents worldwide. The goal is to improve associate proficiency and performance, including sales, across global contact centers as part of enterprise adoption. Julie Stone notes that AI alone doesn't drive performance. Translating AI insights into everyday behaviors through realistic practice and timely coaching is essential. The approach links immersive AI-powered practice, performance intelligence, and closed-loop coaching to convert AI insights into daily tasks and repeatable results, eliminating silos between learning, coaching, and execution. The ecosystem centers on RealSkill and Perform to create a continuous skills and behavior framework from onboarding through optimization, with a real-time view of frontline effectiveness. Client examples show tangible outcomes, including a 10% sales increase for an insurance firm and 17-23% NPS boosts for healthcare and telecom clients through data-informed coaching. It positions AI-enabled capabilities as industry agnostic, bridging AI investments with real business outcomes through ongoing enterprise adoption and scale. Measured outcomes include roughly 12% improvement in associate retention, 6 to 8% reductions in average handle time, 100% compliance accuracy within 60 days, a 23% rise in Net Promoter Score, and a 10% lift in sales conversion rates. TTEC highlights a global CX technology and services approach focused on AI-enabled digital CX, spanning AI, analytics, CRM, and omnichannel capabilities, with a continuous feedback loop mapping AI simulations to live coaching skills. The overall message is a cohesive AI deployment that translates automated insights into everyday frontline performance across diverse."], [3, "Industries."], [2, "This has been your Daily Brief in AI."], [1, "To read more about these stories, follow."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["artificial intelligence", "healthcare technology", "life sciences", "biotechnology", "cloud computing", "digital transformation", "data science", "education technology", "open source", "large language models", "machine learning", "financial services", "customer experience", "enterprise software", "marketing analytics"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778519527.7008893, "duration_sec": 2.927060842514038, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[4, "I think it's completely normal for someone leaving their home country and accessing healthcare to be questioning, you know, is this is this safe? What are the standards of care in this different country? And then I think there's another layer on top of that where I think they're also trying to combat really prejudiced assumptions about Mexico as much as possible, that it's, you know, a country from the Global South, that it's underdeveloped or even undeveloped, that there's no way the standards could be as high of quality in a place like Mexico."], [1, "Cristina says that clinics in Algodones employ certain strategies to maintain a positive reputation with tourists. Some clinics will pay medical tourism companies in other countries to act as a middleman between patient and clinic. Many of these companies are based in Europe, and they'll put Mexican clinics through a vetting process to prove their legitimacy to a non-Mexican clientele. When making interior design choices, clinics might consider an American aesthetic, whatever that means. The goal is to make sure the image of Mexican dental care is squeaky clean. Sometimes quite literally. Cristina remembers being hit by the intense smell of sanitizer when she walked into the many clinics in town."], [4, "[Speaker:Cristina] It was overwhelming sometimes walking into a dental clinic, just the smell of sanitization. Like, it feels like above and beyond anything I'd ever experienced myself walking into healthcare spaces. Like, they're really trying to make sure you smell and notice right away, like, this is clean."], [1, "[Speaker:Lulu] All this effort is to make Los Algodones feel safe. And comfortable, relatable even. And so there are ways in which the town has worked to play up its Americanness by minimizing its Mexicanness."], [4, "I definitely remember this sense of, like, staff being told to minimize their Mexican accents because that would help people, tourists, feel more comfortable."], [1, "Christina's research also found that some clinics would hire someone who speaks American English to just sit in the waiting room. The idea being that their presence might help nervous Americans feel more at ease. It seems like everyone who lives and works in town has their own strategy to calm anxious American nerves. One local dental tourism company uses a camera to occasionally livestream the border crossing from a bird's-eye view. These videos exist almost as if to say, \"See? It's so\u2014 uneventful here. Boring, in fact.\" safe. Back when Dr. Jesus Medina was head of the tourism department in the '80s, he developed his own strategy to make skeptical US tourists feel comfortable in a Mexican dental clinic. He created a complaint office, a place tourists could go if they felt like they were being scammed. He called it the Office of Defense of the tourists."], [2, "That way, if one Mexican doctor do a bad job to one of the patients, they can go to that office, you know? And then we call the doctor and we make them give the money back to the patients, you know?"], [1, "The office functioned like an amateur courtroom. And Medina enlisted Dr. Maga\u00f1a as the judge. Maga\u00f1a would inspect the dental work of other Mexican dentists \u2014occasionally calling the police in for backup."], [2, "It was very hard, you know. We seen the police and comes there. He says\u2014and Mariana says\u2014your work is not good. You have to give the money back to the lady."], [1, "Police in Los Algodones exist to protect the industry and to make tourists feel safe. There's an old promotional video for Molar City still up on YouTube. It's from 12 years ago, and it's less than a minute long. In the clip, a police officer does a salsa twirl with an elderly white woman. The video is titled \"All Tourists Are Safe and Fun in Los Lagodones.\"."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["medical tourism", "dental care", "healthcare quality", "cross border travel", "mexico", "united states", "patient trust", "clinic reputation", "sanitation", "tourism industry", "cultural identity", "stereotyping", "language accent", "consumer protection", "police security"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778519718.0687404, "duration_sec": 1.734241247177124, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And look, if this episode has you thinking about the security of your own stack, that's exactly the right instinct. That's where the Bitcoin Way comes in. Your personal Bitcoin IT team, self-custody training, cybersecurity, Plan B residency, over 20 years of expertise. They simplify the whole thing so that you stay in complete control of your stack. Book a free 30-minute consultation at thebitcoinway.com slash simply. Tell them Satoshi sent you."], [0, "Here's the theory I want to put forward and I want to be transparent in that this is my opinion, not an established fact, but I think it's worth saying out loud. Building a quantum computer capable of breaking Bitcoin's cryptography is not something an individual, a startup, or even most corporations can do."], [0, "We're talking about a machine requiring hundreds of thousands of physical quibits operating at temperatures colder than outer space, requiring extraordinary cooling infrastructure, billions of dollars of investment and years of coordinated engineering at a nation state level. The entities that can build this are Google, government research labs and state sponsored programs like China's Quantum Initiative."], [0, "These are not decentralized actors. These are the most centralized, most powerful institutions on the planet. And here's the thing about those institutions. They need centralized currency to maintain control. The ability to print money, to freeze accounts, to monitor financial transactions, to cut people off from the financial system. That is a fundamental pillar of how modern states exercise power. And Bitcoin is a direct threat to that pillar."], [0, "A thick supply, permissionless, censorship-resistant monetary network that nobody controls is the last thing that a centralized power structure wants to see succeed. So ask yourself, what is the incentive structure here? The most powerful"], [0, "Powerful centralized actors on the planet have both the means to build quantum computers and the motive to use them against the most powerful decentralized monetary network ever created. Quantum isn't just a technical threat to Bitcoin. It's the most powerful tool a centralized system has to fight back against a decentralized one. And yet Bitcoin has survived 17 years of existential threats."], [0, "code bugs, exchange hacks, regulatory crackdowns, forks, bands, bear markets. Every single time the network adapted and got stronger. So here's what I find genuinely fascinating about Bitcoin and it connects to something bigger than just quantum. Bitcoin is, in a very real sense, the first network in history where human beings have a direct economic incentive to ensure its survival"], [0, "and upgrade its defenses. Miners, developers, node operators. They're not doing this out of charity. The Bitcoin they hold, mine, and earn only has value if the network survives. The halving creates a perpetual economic engine that funds security indefinitely."], [0, "The open source development model means that when a threat is identified, like quantum, the best cryptographers in the world have every incentive to solve it. BIP 360 being on testnet right now is not an accident. It's Bitcoin's immune system activating. The same way that the network adapted to SegWit, to Taproot, to Lightning, it will adapt to quantum. Not because someone told it to, because the incentive structure demands it."], [0, "And here's the other thing, post-quantum cryptography doesn't just protect Bitcoin. SHA-256 and quantum resistance signature schemes will eventually be the security backbone of the entire digital economy. Banking systems, government databases, military communications, Bitcoin is not fighting this battle alone. The entire internet has to solve this problem."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["bitcoin", "blockchain", "cryptocurrency", "cryptography", "quantum computing", "cybersecurity", "network security", "self custody", "decentralization", "government", "monetary policy", "financial privacy", "open source", "digital economy", "incentive structure"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778520347.8215032, "duration_sec": 1.963423728942871, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "And at that time you were already training with Maurice Smith."], [1, "Yes, Maurice was one of the guests."], [0, "Right, you were training. Yeah. Because I ended up training with Maurice Smith, you know, every time I'd go to Seattle. You know, we'd train together. So, you know, we're like, part of this like kind of karate martial art nerd culture."], [1, "Yeah."], [0, "When it wasn't even popular."], [1, "No."], [0, "I used to see you all the time. You know, you and, you know, doing jiu-jitsu, Carl Parisian and all these guys at Legends. Where was another place? It was, we had, there was Legends and there was another place."], [1, "The Bomb Squad."], [0, "Yes."], [1, "The Bomb Squad was the first place that Eddie taught at and then that place closed down, then we went to Legends. Yeah. And then we moved to the other Legends that was like in more East LA. Mm-hmm. And then And then Eddie started opening up his own place in downtown."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["martial arts", "karate", "jiu jitsu", "training", "combat sports", "gym", "fighting", "athletes", "coaching", "martial arts culture", "seattle", "los angeles", "martial arts history", "community"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778520345.1642838, "duration_sec": 7.688483238220215, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "named_entities_extraction", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "named_entities_extraction", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "document", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "September 24, 2025\nA regular meeting of the City Council of the City of Boston was held in the Christopher A. Iannella\nChamber, City Hall on Wednesday, September 24, 2025, at 12:03 P.M.\nPresident Louijeune in the Chair. Present Councilors Breadon, Coletta Zapata, Durkan, FitzGerald,\nFlynn, Mejia, Murphy, Pep\u00e9n, Santana, and Weber. Absent Councilor Worrell.\nReverend Father Alexandros Pretorian of St. Nectarios Greek Orthodox Church, delivered the\ninvocation, and the meeting was opened with the pledge of allegiance.\nThe Chair moved to adopt the minutes from the September 17, 2025 City Council Meeting.\nMotion prevailed.\n_________________________________________________________________________\n1690 Message and order for your approval an Order authorizing the City of\nBoston to appropriate the amount of Two Million Five Hundred Thousand\nDollars ($2,500,000.00) for the purpose of paying the cost of a feasibility\nstudy and schematic design associated with a project for the Ruth Batson\nAcademy,"]], "participants": null}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "ner_001", "crc": 1321322, "title": "Extract named entities from documents", "name": "extract_named_entities", "description": "Identifies and extracts named entities from provided documents.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "ner", "prompt_template": "Extract all named entities from the provided document. Include the following entity types: PERSON, ORGANIZATION, LOCATION, and any other relevant entities. Return as comma-delimited list of entities with their types (e.g., 'John Smith (PERSON), ACM Corp (ORGANIZATION)). Only return the entities without any additional commentary.", "output_variable": "extracted_entities", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["city council of the city of boston", "city of boston", "boston city hall", "christopher a. iannella chamber", "president louijeune", "councilor breadon", "councilor coletta zapata", "councilor durkan", "councilor fitzgerald", "councilor flynn", "councilor mejia", "councilor murphy", "councilor pep\u00e9n", "councilor santana", "councilor weber", "councilor worrell", "reverend father alexandros pretorian", "st. nectarios greek orthodox church", "ruth batson academy", "$2", "500", "000 appropriation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778520376.0209653, "duration_sec": 1.7544379234313965, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Is that a problem? Probably it is, right? And so sometimes that's the funny thing about telemetry is that people tend to zoom all the way in. We were talking about trace IDs. What is the individual request? And a lot of the time that's very important, but sometimes it's actually really important to have that 10,000 foot view where you say, just what is the lay of the land? What does it all look like as a picture? And that's also..."], [0, "Something that's like not that easy to do now. There's not a lot of standard stuff for that and like way or just best practices, you know, like how do you set up your dashboarding and, you know, if you're using Prometheus or if you're using Datadog or whatever it is, you know, like how do you like using that is like a big."], [0, "failing, I think, in DevOps teams and traditional teams today is making sure that you always go back to your telemetry and say, why didn't this tell us about this problem before it happened? There should be almost like a root cause analysis after issues. And it doesn't have to be this fancy process, but just to say, why were we not aware of this now that we understand what it was?"], [0, "Yeah, that's such a really good point, especially like large teams, large companies. If they haven't thought about telemetry in the beginning, you know, they want to introduce it at a later time, but they don't know where to begin. And normally detection really helps them to explore the area as well. It's not super obvious to you, but you can like maybe run it just to see and like explore all these like edge cases and some of the critical things like correlations. And, you know, it may actually help you to explore what you need to take a look at."], [0, "even if you end up having a SLO type of approach in the end. Yeah, I mean, you said the most important word, which is correlations. And a lot of the time, it's not obvious to the human eye, but it can really help when you're trying to scale systems. And the nature of scale has changed so much now. You can scale up easily nowadays in the cloud or in containers or whatever, but the difficulty and the challenges at the languages that we write in are so high up the stack"], [0, "That like a lot of the time, the difficulty in diagnosing like the bottlenecks or the performance issues of things can be very hard. And like being able to put two data points together and understand that that's where something is slow or like that's why it's never going to scale. Even if you put 3000 servers behind it, that can be helped a lot by anomaly detection. Yeah, this is more art than science, it sounds like."], [0, "It really is. I read this funny thing the other day about the difference with developers. Some are artists and some are engineers, I think, or whatever it was. But it is like an art because you really need to say, well, what are the what-ifs? And what can I just store and see what I get out of it? It is experimental, I think."], [0, "Okay. One last question for you before we go to our next segment. Based on what you're seeing so far and how your customers are, what you're seeing in terms of the data that you can glean from how your customers use your product, are most of the things that trigger something to look at, right? Something that's important to look at, right? Of the anomalies."], [0, "Are most of those being triggered from internal sources, meaning that the developer is pushing new code, making changes that's causing issues, or are those coming from the outside, right? Maybe there's somebody who's trying to brute force their way in, or maybe the company just got listed on some popular website or something, and maybe there's a surge in traffic. What is it, generally speaking, where are the biggest sources of problems?"], [0, "Generally speaking, I mean, you know, like the answer I want to give you as well, you know, it's both. But let's pin me down for an actual answer. If my back was against the wall, I'd say usually it's the servers and the apps that fail in a condition which the team has struggled to test."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["telemetry", "observability", "monitoring", "distributed tracing", "metrics", "dashboards", "devops", "anomaly detection", "correlation", "performance", "scalability", "cloud computing", "incident response", "root cause analysis", "service reliability"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778520448.2855527, "duration_sec": 2.0563302040100098, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "That's all for me too. I mean, I'm just as passionate about, you know, Aerosmith as I am about the Isley Brothers. But I've never looked at life as I have to think in this parameter. You know, I've got to be marginalized. That's just\u2014 man, come on. That's such a waste of life."], [1, "It is."], [0, "It's all for you, man. So\u2014 I agree. With the martial arts and everything else, I look at every martial art. Just like everything else, everything has something to contribute. Just like all people have something to contribute. Even an idiot, you can learn from an idiot."], [1, "You can, you can. A lot of idiots say wise things occasionally."], [0, "Yeah, because everybody's going to have a quotient of legitimacy. Maybe it may be 20% and they don't see the 80%, but until you acknowledge that 20%, they're not gonna hear you. You know what I mean? So that's the thing. Man, we're on this planet, and one of the things I meant, I don't envy a whole lot of people, but dude, I do envy you because you get to expand your world. You talk to so many interesting people, and that's, what a great thing. What a great thing to just have all these type of perspectives and all that coming through. And I gotta say, man, I'm super proud of you 'cause I know you as Joe from the gym, and look what you've done, man. Man, it's like that's a shot in the arm because it's like people that you like and seeing them prosper, that's cool as shit."], [1, "Yeah, I've learned a lot, man. And I didn't expect to, you know, when we first started doing this, it was just for fun. We just get together with our friends, you know."], [0, "But you knew what you wanted to do, man. You were pretty damn clear because do you remember this? You remember me coming to, I think it was the Ice House?"], [1, "In Pasadena?"], [0, "Yeah. No, no, no. Oh shoot. It wasn't Ice House. It was in It was in Orange County. I came to see\u2014 Comedy Magic Club, maybe? I came to see you perform, and I offered you the role in Blood and Bone. Do you remember that at all?"], [1, "I do! Yeah, yeah, I do now. Yeah, okay."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["music", "rock", "soul", "aerosmith", "isley brothers", "martial arts", "self improvement", "life philosophy", "open mindedness", "learning", "personal growth", "perspective", "comedy", "podcasting", "acting"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778521061.7954934, "duration_sec": 1.9282417297363281, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "What a wonderful guy. So that's one thing about fighting, you, you can't hide your nature, you know what I mean? You\u2014 people see who you are, and he was, he was a wonderful human being. But like, but like a lot of people, like almost like street basketball as opposed to, you know, professional, you miss out on certain techniques that you need when you're trying to step up. Right. And so like, well, Kimbo, you know, he would, like a lot of people, he would kind of telegraph. And so when we were shooting the movie, you know, And I basically\u2014 we had a cameraman that did not really know how to shoot stuff, so I just had to do everything on screen. And so I would\u2014 I just wanted to make everything very realistic. And so Kimbo had this rubber knife, and I was like, try your best to touch me with the rubber knife. And so he would try, but before\u2014 but as soon as he would move, there'd be a little bit of an indication that I'd see, and then I throw the punch and it would go really close to him, and I have him react to that. But he was going, \"Wait a minute, how are you hitting me before I can get this knife out?\" And then I told him, you know, I'll show you what that is later. Because, you know, kind of like, not to be real nerdish about it, but like, why are like 50 and 60-year-old trainers meeting people's hands, like a 20-year-old guy's or contender's hands, like this. You see the person with the pad moving just as much as the other guy because there's an indication. There's\u2014 they do this beforehand. They're always kind of flexing and going in reverse before they go forward. So just for over years, I wouldn't do that and I would exploit that, you know. So it's kind of like a cheat code that I'm like, hell, what the hell, what the hell I'm gonna do with it? I'm, I'm an actor. So my thing is, just like yourself, when I see you, you know, George St-Pierre and how we are always in the gym, we're, you know, we're kind of collaborating. We're, you know, we're, we're\u2014 it's about just getting better, not no ego or anything else like that. It's just like, hey man, we're like kind You know, kind of like jamming on technique and getting better."], [1, "Well, especially if you run into someone who has a different style than you. Yeah, because there's always something in different styles that you could take out of it."], [0, "Absolutely."], [1, "There's always something, and we're seeing that now. There's all these different martial artists that are entering into MMA that have these different techniques that people haven't seen before, and there's a lot of them that people dismiss that you're finding are very effective. Especially if you don't know how to do them, you don't know what they are. So you don't\u2014 you have like a database in your mind of movements. Like, I'm sure you see when a guy's loading up on a spin. Oh yeah, everybody sees that. But if you don't know that, you don't see it, right?"], [0, "And if you, if you were loading up, then you're not gonna, you're not gonna capitalize on it, right? Because you don't, you know, you're taking\u2014 there's a millisecond that you're taking because Your movement is not efficient."], [1, "This episode is brought to you by ThreatLocker. Here's the truth: reacting to cyber attacks isn't enough. You need control from the start. That's where ThreatLocker comes in. With zero trust, you only allow what you need, blocking everything else by default. You're in control what runs, when, where, and how. Ransomware, zero-day exploits, Block them before they can execute. And now with AI now increasing the speed and volume of attacks, a fast response isn't fast enough. Visit threatlocker.com/jre to learn more. There's a move that still to this day people aren't doing effectively when someone loads up, because you could see the load up and it's just a jam. It's just lifting your foot up and put it on the hips. And it's super effective in Taekwondo because everybody's fast, you know, everybody's trying to do that technique. But that jam of just lifting your foot up and just not trying to hit them hard, just putting that foot on the hip, it fucks people up. And I don't see anybody using that right now."], [0, "I tell you, man, like, I don't\u2014 like, as in life, there's always something that you can gain from the\u2014 you know, people want to\u2014 I don't know, people are in their own egos a lot of times. But like, even wushu, Me, it was hard as hell for me doing wushu against guys half my size. It's not against, but it's a performance thing. But if I can do all of it, can go to these very hard techniques of like, I gotta get down to the floor and I gotta\u2014 Body mastery. Yeah, at my size. Well then, I'm better. So if I want to kick you in the eyebrow, I can. Right. Because I'm\u2014 it's about, you know, having my body do what my mind's telling it to. Right. And so, but of course people want to dismiss it because, oh, that ain't real, you can't use it. Yeah, good. Yeah. Just like ballet is hard as hell, you can't use that either. But anybody, any heavyweight who put themselves through ballet would be a better fighter."], [1, "100%. Look at Lomachenko."], [0, "Yeah."], [1, "His dad taught him Ukrainian dance. Lomachenko's dad pulled him out of boxing for 2 years when he was young."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["martial arts", "mma", "fighting", "boxing", "training", "technique", "movement", "footwork", "taekwondo", "wushu", "dance", "ballet", "athletic performance", "body control", "self improvement"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778521103.4024265, "duration_sec": 2.8720622062683105, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Was on Friar Street."], [1, "Yep, yep, yep."], [0, "Right down from the Goosens."], [1, "Yep. Yeah, right down."], [0, "Yeah."], [1, "And, um, that, that was an honor, man, to be able to train in that gym. That was incredible. It was incredible."], [0, "Oh yeah, yeah, I used to be there. Yeah, yeah, man. Yeah, those are some great times because, I mean, I connect with Benny where\u2014 because when I was in Bridgeport, my instructor Manny Malice went to California and started training with Benny. Oh wow, they're early on. So he put that on the map about coming to the mecca and training with Benny."], [1, "It was the mecca for kickboxing, especially in the '90s. That was the mecca. You had to go to the Jet Center."], [0, "Everyone talked about the Jet Center. Yeah, man, that was\u2014 So we were."], [1, "Always wondering, like, what is the style? What's the best style? And then the UFC came along and I'm like, oh my God, they did it. They did it. They figured it out. They put it all together. And for a while it was jiu-jitsu because nobody understood jiu-jitsu and Royce Gracie was just running shit."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["martial arts", "kickboxing", "mixed martial arts", "ufc", "jiu jitsu", "training", "gym", "fighting styles", "combat sports", "boxing", "fitness", "sports history", "california", "bridgeport", "1990s"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778521549.8283105, "duration_sec": 1.7157964706420898, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So Microsoft and Apple have both said that they will be developing ES8 plugins that are similar to Mozilla's, just slightly different with some syntax things, etc. Except with functionalities that they have decided to add that will better serve their user base. So for example, Spartan will provide a WYSIWYG API."], [0, "that can transform any DOM element into an editable Microsoft Word widget. And Apple's version of ES8 will only be executed by sites that have deployed their JavaScript applications through their open web store. And this is another new thing that Apple is going to probably bring to the table next year. And the way it works is just like you can sign up to be a developer of..."], [0, "iOS applications or a developer of Mac applications, you can now sign up to be a developer of web applications. And these applications will be executable in the browser, including in Safari. So if you want your web applications to work in Safari, you're going to need to go and check out that subscription service that Apple will most likely be providing. And that will give you access to ECMAScript 8."], [0, "So ESA will also provide a native implementation of parallax and a scroll point API that will allow even beginner developers to fundamentally change how the scroll bar works. So I know that a lot of people are really excited about this one because, uh, it's sometimes it's really difficult to get a parallax working, even though there are so many implementations out there that have already done it. So, uh,"], [0, "So this API is going to make it just a drop-in, like a plug-in, kind of like jQuery, except it's going to be native. Now, I'm really excited about this. It's going to increase the quality of user experience, and everybody's website is going to work in a unique, new way. And you'll never know what the scroll bar is going to do when you go from one website to the next, because everybody's going to be implementing new scrolling functionality."], [0, "Another powerful and important feature that I'm looking forward to in these ES8 plugins is that each and every feature of ES8 will be optional for users. So in a configuration section, whenever you are installing the ES8 plugin, you'll be able to decide"], [0, "which pieces of ESA you would like to be enabled for your browser. This level of granular control will make each and every browser different, which is just an incredible advancement in web development."], [0, "Now, as a front-end developer, you might be worried that this will be difficult to deal with because everybody's browser will have different functionality. But luckily, what ES8 is going to do is modify the user agent string so that all of the functionalities that the user has enabled will be in that user string. So all you have to do is just check that user agent string to see what functionality the user has enabled or disabled."], [0, "So last but not least, the folks at Twitter have teamed up with all of the major providers of this different ESA plugins to create a way for users to constantly share their camera and audio feeds with the world transparently."], [0, "So anytime a user opens their browser, if they have an ES8 plugin installed, it will automatically and transparently share the location, the video feed, and the audio feed of that user's device with the world using WebRTC."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["web development", "javascript", "ecmascript", "browser plugins", "microsoft", "apple", "safari", "dom manipulation", "wysiwyg editor", "user experience", "scrolling", "parallax", "user agent", "webrtc", "privacy"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778521988.6990538, "duration_sec": 3.2507824897766113, "validator_hotkey": "5GQyFzvtVMw9X45t6dvUFgR9unFpDCccPPCiAoqJrtPuC8my", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah, so this is cool. Actually, I did a whole write-up on this. I think it was the same report. But 62% of users report having access to data they shouldn't. 43% of businesses need more than a month to detect people accessing stuff they're not supposed to. And these are all my summaries too, right? These are very casual sentences. It's because I wrote the summary. San says only 9% are happy with their insider threat controls."], [0, "Mimecast says 45% of executives say malicious insiders is the email risk they're least ready for. Oh, and the Grok says that the equation group insider threat option is lame. So the Grok does not believe"], [0, "This is different than a previous story, by the way, just kind of blending them together. But the Gruck does not believe that the equation group leak was another Snowden. He doesn't believe it's an NSA insider. Basically, his belief is that it was a jump box that got compromised and had too much stuff on it. So that was InfoSec News."], [0, "Moving on to technology news and articles. Alphabet is launching a ride sharing service. So Alphabet is what we all used to call Google, but now Google is a subset of Alphabet. So Google is Alphabet. I'll get used to that eventually. Hopefully they won't change their name again."], [0, "but they're launching a ride sharing service, but it's different. There's going to compete with Uber, but it's different. So here's how it's different. It's actually, it's Waze integrated. So when you're doing Waze and you're going from here to there, it knows where you're going because you've put it in a destination. Well, it looks for other people along the way and you basically do ride sharing. So it's like people doing what they normally do"], [0, "but adding a rideshare component that's integrated directly into Waze, which you're already using anyway, which I think is fascinating. I wonder how they're gonna deal with the issue of like, are you using Google Maps or are you using Waze? It's like Google Maps is competing with Waze and Google Maps does not have the ridesharing option, but maybe they'll clean that up with an integration or a unification or something."], [0, "But pretty interesting that Uber is going to have some competition from Alphabet on this in a related but different model. Tesla teasing product announcement, which is a solar roof. Man, Tesla, I fucking love Tesla."], [0, "They're just doing the most amazing shit. So they're completely revolutionizing. By the way, sorry for the cussing. If anyone cares about cussing, I think I'm going to keep doing it. And I'm trying not to go overboard with it. But I probably have some listeners who are Christian or might have kids in the car. And I apologize about that. But I don't think I want to cut it out. So..."], [0, "Hopefully, that's not an issue. If it is actually email me and like, I'll consider making adjustment. Truthfully, I will. But yeah, continuing on with Tesla. So"], [0, "First of all, the cars, okay? They kind of force the issue and everyone's doing electric cars and it's going really well and their new models coming out are looking amazing, especially the super affordable one. Talking about battery tech upgrades that can get us, you know, 300, 400 miles on a charge. Talking about battery tech upgrades that get us like 300 miles on a few minutes of a charge. I think like Volkswagen's working on that for like 2019."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Technology"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["cybersecurity", "insider threat", "data access", "risk management", "email security", "breach detection", "technology news", "alphabet", "ride sharing", "navigation apps", "waze", "uber", "tesla", "electric vehicles", "solar energy"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778522466.532761, "duration_sec": 3.262277841567993, "validator_hotkey": "5C8LULGoFybR7ybg4S8qQkaaowGXFFja4KmMoSqwhxuAsn33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "A mob boss?"], [1, "Yeah, he was a dangerous, dangerous guy. He was the guy that that movie that Leonardo DiCaprio starred in with Jack Nicholson. What was that movie, Jamie? Remember that movie that was based on Whitey Bulger? The Departed? Yeah, yeah, yeah. Okay, that was based on Whitey Bulger."], [0, "He was a gangster?"], [1, "Oh, yeah. Terrifying gangster."], [0, "Yeah. What was his demise?"], [1, "He was actually a fucking FBI informant. Not only was he a gangster, he was working with the FBI, and they were letting him get away with shit because he was throwing other people under the bus."], [0, "I think there's a different\u2014 I mean\u2014."], [1, "They wound up catching him in Santa Monica. Well, I've found that\u2014 Hunting Whitey. About that Whitey Bulger won the Mass Millions lottery about that time. Yeah. Yeah, he won the fucking lottery, man. So this is what it says here. What does it say? I think it was more of a scheme than they actually won, but it's always taking money. It's probably a way to like launder money."], [0, "Yeah."], [1, "Oh, 100%. It's a way to launder money. So the way it would work was like, say if you lived in the community and you won the lottery ticket, maybe they would give you money for your lottery ticket, then he would get it, and that way it would show that this is where he got his income from. Like, these guys would all own businesses, right? But the reason why they would own businesses is so they could show why they drive a Cadillac, why they have a mansion, why they have this\u2014 because they have legitimate businesses. But really, these businesses were scams."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["organized crime", "mob", "gangsters", "whitey bulger", "fbi informant", "crime history", "money laundering", "lottery", "corruption", "criminal investigation", "film references", "undercover work", "illegal businesses", "boston crime", "fraud"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778522563.3469322, "duration_sec": 1.9325051307678223, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Bandwidth for changelog is provided by Fastly. Learn more at fastly.com. We move fast and fix things here at changelog because of Rollbar. Check them out at rollbar.com. And we're hosted on Linode servers. Head to linode.com slash changelog."], [0, "This episode is sponsored by our friends at Rollbar. How important is it for you to catch errors before your users do? What if you could resolve those errors in minutes and then deploy with confidence? That's exactly what Rollbar enables for software teams. One of the most frustrating things we all deal with is errors. Most teams either A rely on their users to report errors or B use log files and lists of errors to debug problems."], [0, "That's such a waste of time. Instantly know what's broken and why with Rollbar. Reduce time wasted debugging and automatically capture errors alongside rich diagnostic data to help you defeat impactful errors. You can integrate Rollbar into your existing workflow. It integrates with your source code repository and deployment system to give you deep insights into exactly what changes caused each error."], [0, "Give Rollbar a try today at no cost to you. No credit card is required. Our listeners get access to the Bootstrap plan with 100,000 events for free for 90 days. To get started, head to rollbar.com slash changelog."], [0, "Welcome to JS Party, a weekly celebration of JavaScript and the web. Tune in live on Thursdays at 1 p.m. U.S. Eastern at changelog.com slash live. Join the community and snag with us in real time during the shows at the changelog.com slash community. Follow us on Twitter. We're at jspartyfm. And now on to the show."], [0, "Hello, and welcome to another episode of JS Party, where it's a party every week with JavaScript. I'll be your host today, Nick Nisi, and I'm joined with my fellow panelists, Suze Hinton. Hey, Suze. Hey, how's it going? It's good to be back. Good to be back with you as well. And our other panelist is Jared Santo. Hello. Excited to chat with y'all. Dev tools, debugging, fun stuff."], [0, "Yeah, lots of fun and very practical for everyone. So let's dig right into it today. We thought we'd talk about debugging and some DevTools magic and kind of how we approach that part of the job of developing with JavaScript and how we manage bugs or deal with bugs and some cool features that the platforms provide us. So I guess I'll start off with a basic question. How do you use..."], [0, "How do you get into debugging? Like you have a bug that you don't really know much information about. What are kind of the first steps that you take? Why don't we start with you, Suze? Yeah, I start super, super basic. So usually when I'm developing something that's more on the front end side in the browser, I'll usually just have dev tools popped up at the bottom, like always on showing the console tab."], [0, "And then whenever I'm sort of developing a feature and sort of manually testing it, I'll usually just look for an error. And then if the error shows up, which it usually does, right, because we're not all perfect the first time when we code something, if the error is super, super obvious and there's like a line of code, then I'll just go straight back to my code and fix it. But if it's something that I don't know what it is,"], [0, "Generally, I'll Google it really quickly first. And if I don't really find anything that's specific to my case, that's when I sort of start digging deeper and deeper. And so what I love about the dev tools in every single browser that I've worked with is that it gives you the line number. You can generally click on the error and it will take you to that source code. And then that's where I'll set a break point and start stepping through, you know, refresh the page and start stepping through. So mine definitely starts super high level because I find that even though"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["javascript", "web development", "debugging", "developer tools", "browser console", "breakpoints", "error tracking", "logging", "source code", "software testing", "troubleshooting", "frontend development", "deployment", "workflow integration", "performance monitoring"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
