 but older than Janet Mills. Thanks for the call, Pat, and don't be a stranger. Don't be a stranger, okay? 844-500-4242. Pat, Phillip, I've got about 30 seconds here. Go ahead. Hi, I'm Howie. I just wanted to briefly add with Graham Plattner. He was stationed in Croatia."], [0, "And he said he got that tattoo there. During World War II, the Ustasha ran a concentration camp in Croatia, and they also used that symbol. Yeah. I know. And he tells different stories. He's a history buff, but he didn't know what it meant. And now he says, well, I did know what it meant, but I had PTSD. So which is it? Get your story straight there, Oyster Fuhrer. I'm Howie Carr."], [0, "Live from the Berna Less Lethal Pistol Studio, it's Howie Carr. I'm watching a trumpet from the villages. He's really on fire. He's having a good time this afternoon. Talking about the cognitive tests."], [0, "And everything else. I don't know. I don't know how he does it. I mean, he's got to make another speech tonight in West Palm. I just don't, I don't know how he can keep going like that. You know, it's not, you know, it's not like he's just making some, you know, pro forma remarks. You know, he's like really just, you know, doing a stand-up routine here."], [0, "for uh for the voters in the villages which obviously one of his it's it's a big trump area but you know he just he's almost 80 and he keeps keeps going anyway i want to read some of this story did we uh did we uh yes it's been uh yep it's been posted on x yep"], [0, "And this is a good story, and if you want to learn more about this guy, this is a good way to just get a sampling of his absurdity. The Absurd Rise of Graham Plattner. It's by Jim Garrity, National Review Online. And he says he's been joking about it."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["News & Politics"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["politics", "elections", "gerrymandering", "congressional seats", "voting districts", "republican party", "democratic party", "florida legislature", "ron desantis", "donald trump", "campaign rallies", "political commentary", "history", "world war ii", "concentration camps"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778532476.1244097, "duration_sec": 1.6765344142913818, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Still a huge improvement, though. And I'd say in practice, I think most of the time, at least when I use pattern matching, it's usually just two or three matches. So two would be if I have an option or a result, then your matches are success, failure, or failure."], [0, "Well, there's no third case in that case, but usually it's just one or two. And then you always have an else match case, like what to do when none of the other matches match. I assume you would use it with enums a lot, where you have multiple"], [0, "options within your enum and you essentially just want to like go through each of the possible options and then, you know, perform some action or whatever. I like it. It's better than like if statements generally. When I heard that Russ had pattern matching, I do a lot of Elixir still day in, day out. And so I was like, this is great. You know, I love pattern matching. And I was like, oh, this is. Yeah. Underwhelming to say the least when you come from those kind of languages."], [0, "There's some really cool stuff you can do. But yeah, with those Erlang elixir, those being more dynamic, you can definitely do, like you said, more stuff. But yeah, it definitely does kind of make things more interesting rather than just saying if else, because you can kind of get yourself out of there. And then they have a form of guard clause where they say, I think, I don't remember what the keyword is. Is it also when or is there something else?"], [0, "Yeah, you can have guards. You can basically just have an if statement. So match on this thing if this condition is true. And it's just an ordinary if statement. So you can put anything in there. It does help a lot because there's some things you want to express. But still, nonetheless, I wish there was a way that you could pattern match at the function head level, just kind of dry up some code."], [0, "Yeah, the way that works in Elixir, where you can essentially have the same function signature with the match in the function. I mean, that's basically just syntax sugar, but it's really nice. It's a very nice way of flattening the code out, you could say. It would be cool if Rust had that. I don't think that that's something Rust would have, though, because it doesn't"], [0, "The reason it wouldn't, I don't think that would happen. Or maybe, I mean, I don't know. Maybe someday. But the reason it doesn't make sense is just that Rust is intended to be somewhat C compatible in a way. And that's not a very C-like thing. Functions don't really work like that in C. But I don't know. Who knows? Maybe Rust will one day add something like that"], [0, "Yeah. I mean, I could see that happening because, I mean, the way that Elixir does it in Erlang, it is, like you said, some kind of sugar. I heard it's basically implemented with a bunch of case statements. So, I mean, under the hood, it may just generate a bunch of functions since we already do, like, I mean, I think it's possible to do because we already do the generics and we kind of take a look to see which forms are being created. So..."], [0, "I can see definitely there probably is a way to do that. But yeah, I think that a typical Rust developer, this wouldn't be idiomatic Rust at this point right now. You know, people would be like, what is this? It would definitely be something that would just, you know, I don't know, it would just blow your mind. It blew my mind the first few times I ran into it. But then, yeah, I think it would be just such a shock for so many people. I don't know about how you feel."], [0, "I mean, I think it would be nice. But like I said, I think the purists would be like, well, you can't do that because you're secretly generating multiple functions. And then how is that going to work in my world? But I don't know. I mean, you can write macros that"]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["pattern matching", "rust", "elixir", "erlang", "functional programming", "programming languages", "syntax sugar", "guard clauses", "enums", "option type", "result type", "if statements", "macros", "generics", "c language"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
