{"timestamp": 1778533950.719167, "duration_sec": 3.3451061248779297, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "in GitHub from Sentry, then somebody else can come in and fix it, push that code up to GitHub, fix it. We can mark it as change and say, hey, it was fixed in this particular commit. That way, when it breaks again, we know who to blame. So head on to Sentry at .io, use the coupon code tastetreat, all lowercase, all one word, and you will get two months for free. Highly recommend."], [0, "So let's say you had, and by the way, this is no longer the ad transition. So let's say that you no longer, or let's say you wanted to work on some code that already exists in GitHub. And you were wondering, or GitLab or any of these, you're wondering, and you say, huh,"], [0, "I want to play around with this, or I want to try it out, or I have some improvements to help them out with it. What do I do? Well, this is where the next one comes in, which is clone. Now, people who are new to GitHub may head to a specific repository's page. They may see the download button and say, ooh, download. Let me click download. They click the download button and presents them with a modal that shows..."], [0, "Clone with HTTPS along with open and GitHub and then download zip. You're like, OK, well, I'll do the download zip. But before you do that, this is the better way, which is to clone the repo. And we can clone the repo using this string, which is basically the URL to the repo. And you run git clone. You copy and paste the string and you paste it in there, which is the address to the repo. And what does it do?"], [0, "It fetches the entire history as well as the entire repo. So that way you can make modifications to it, add them, commit them and push them back up. And then then we get to do some of the cool, fun stuff that we're going to be talking about next."], [0, "So next we have branches. Branches are key for working on features. Or if you want to, like right now I'm working on my master Gatsby website. And I'm at the same time, I'm also upgrading a couple of the backend components. I'm doing a little bit to the bundlers and the whole site is sort of"], [0, "in development right now however i need to keep like a working copy of the website because sometimes what happens is i'm in the middle of building a feature and then i have to jump back to the main website and fix something really quickly so if i was right now the current branch that you get by default and get is called master but they are talking about changing it to main i think they already did did they"], [0, "I haven't seen that yet. Well, then maybe they haven't, but I, my neural, well, at least my current repos are a main. Oh, cool. Yeah. It was just, there's a github.com forward slash github forward slash renaming, just showing some, some stuff about some tips on, on how to actually change to your main repo instead of mastery. Okay, cool. So if I have everything in my main branch,"], [0, "and I want to work on this feature, I will switch branches and that will allow me to edit all of the files and whatnot. And by switching to a new branch, it basically, it doesn't take a copy, but it gives you your entire website. You can make changes to it. You can add, you can commit, you can even push to your branch on GitHub. However, if you need to go back"], [0, "to what the website was at any time and work on that, it's called checking out. So you can check out to your master branch again, and then you do a quick fix, and then you can jump back to the feature that you're working on. You can check out that branch"]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["git", "github", "version control", "cloning", "branches", "repository", "commit", "push", "checkout", "feature development", "collaboration", "software development", "debugging", "continuous integration", "source code"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778534558.7811005, "duration_sec": 3.6205337047576904, "validator_hotkey": "5DkzbwTnocecNohGP4aNVcHZfUgx8uA1MGQ4WB1u3kfi78gE", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "So we don't get to do Diffie-Hellman in a post-quantum world. We thought we were going to, and then somebody went and completely broke the algorithm that was closest to Diffie-Hellman. Super singular isogenies. We loved them while they lasted."], [0, "And we have these things called chems, which are key exchange mechanisms, which do a thing that's close enough. So just like we used to use Curve25519 to exchange keys and Diffie-Hellman, we can now use things like Kyber, which is now called ML Chem because we can't have nice things. The two things selected were called Kyber and Dilithium."], [0, "such good names and then they went and called them ml chem and ml dsa which one do you want to be saying yeah the star wars star trek names are much better right i'm not even a star wars and star trek fan and i wanted those names nicola do you do you agree with the sentiment did you also prefer these names"], [0, "Yes. And Philippe, what do you think would be the performance impact of using SHA-3 on a common operation, for example, an SSH connection? Do you think it's noticeable? Because we did some benchmark in the past, and we chose to not include SHA-5-1-2, a basic algorithm, because they are close to it."], [0, "So with Shetri, what is the situation?"], [0, "So I think the SHA-512 algorithms were also using a larger Diffie-Hellman group, and that was the very slow one. So the hashes, I think, are not, generally speaking, the slow parts here, except SHA-3 is slow, so it happens to dominate the key exchange step. But to give some order of magnitude, I think that both X25519 and Kyber using SHA-3 take..."], [0, "I don't know, I want to say... Okay, I don't actually remember the actual number, but let's say they take 10. Finite field Diffie-Hellman with a very large group is going to take like 1,000. That's the gap there. There's a hundred-fold difference there. I think SSH is going to be fine."], [0, "Actually, I think that OpenSSH already has a post-quantum key exchange, except that they selected theirs before NIST selected one. So yeah, it's not the one we're implementing in the co-standard library. So hopefully they introduce a Kyber-based one soonish. It seems highly likely that they will, just because that's what everyone else is implementing at this point."], [0, "Yeah. And also because, you know, FIPS 140, you want to be FIPS 140 compliant. Yeah, exactly. FIPS 140 is one of the standards. And it's the one that say it was written for hardware. You know, the things you put in a rack..."], [0, "And it said they have to be sealed and it has to have an LED that does a certain thing. And when you turn it on with a key, it needs to check its circuits to make sure that it's not broken. And then they went and said, yeah, that's the standard you should follow for cryptographic libraries as well. Now, can anybody think how you implement an LED in a library? You just flip a bit. You have a magic bit."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Coding"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["post quantum cryptography", "key exchange", "diffie hellman", "isogeny", "kyber", "dilithium", "sha3", "hashing", "ssh", "openssh", "performance", "benchmarks", "cryptographic standards", "fips 140", "cryptographic libraries"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
