{"timestamp": 1778539438.4762235, "duration_sec": 1.7481703758239746, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "Yeah. Yeah. Random run-in. Yeah, that was crazy. That was crazy. Yeah, man. I was thinking about going there right after this. I'm like, what? Terry Black's? That place was no joke."], [1, "That place rules."], [0, "Yeah, man."], [1, "Are you still in LA?"], [0, "Yeah. Yeah. Yeah."], [1, "What's it like?"], [0, "It's cool, man."], [1, "Is it?"], [0, "Yeah."], [1, "You like it? You're the only person that said that. No."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Comedy"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["travel", "los angeles", "city life", "restaurants", "barbecue", "dining", "food culture", "local favorites", "nightlife", "social encounters", "urban living", "moving", "friends"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
{"timestamp": 1778540012.690472, "duration_sec": 2.6389522552490234, "validator_hotkey": "5FZe9Mpo5dLXfhGZ4VjRnw6dmz9LVPp6nzqPyDfaS9BLKs33", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "testnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[4, "I think it's completely normal for someone leaving their home country and accessing healthcare to be questioning, you know, is this is this safe? What are the standards of care in this different country? And then I think there's another layer on top of that where I think they're also trying to combat really prejudiced assumptions about Mexico as much as possible, that it's, you know, a country from the Global South, that it's underdeveloped or even undeveloped, that there's no way the standards could be as high of quality in a place like Mexico."], [1, "Cristina says that clinics in Algodones employ certain strategies to maintain a positive reputation with tourists. Some clinics will pay medical tourism companies in other countries to act as a middleman between patient and clinic. Many of these companies are based in Europe, and they'll put Mexican clinics through a vetting process to prove their legitimacy to a non-Mexican clientele. When making interior design choices, clinics might consider an American aesthetic, whatever that means. The goal is to make sure the image of Mexican dental care is squeaky clean. Sometimes quite literally. Cristina remembers being hit by the intense smell of sanitizer when she walked into the many clinics in town."], [4, "[Speaker:Cristina] It was overwhelming sometimes walking into a dental clinic, just the smell of sanitization. Like, it feels like above and beyond anything I'd ever experienced myself walking into healthcare spaces. Like, they're really trying to make sure you smell and notice right away, like, this is clean."], [1, "[Speaker:Lulu] All this effort is to make Los Algodones feel safe. And comfortable, relatable even. And so there are ways in which the town has worked to play up its Americanness by minimizing its Mexicanness."], [4, "I definitely remember this sense of, like, staff being told to minimize their Mexican accents because that would help people, tourists, feel more comfortable."], [1, "Christina's research also found that some clinics would hire someone who speaks American English to just sit in the waiting room. The idea being that their presence might help nervous Americans feel more at ease. It seems like everyone who lives and works in town has their own strategy to calm anxious American nerves. One local dental tourism company uses a camera to occasionally livestream the border crossing from a bird's-eye view. These videos exist almost as if to say, \"See? It's so\u2014 uneventful here. Boring, in fact.\" safe. Back when Dr. Jesus Medina was head of the tourism department in the '80s, he developed his own strategy to make skeptical US tourists feel comfortable in a Mexican dental clinic. He created a complaint office, a place tourists could go if they felt like they were being scammed. He called it the Office of Defense of the tourists."], [2, "That way, if one Mexican doctor do a bad job to one of the patients, they can go to that office, you know? And then we call the doctor and we make them give the money back to the patients, you know?"], [1, "The office functioned like an amateur courtroom. And Medina enlisted Dr. Maga\u00f1a as the judge. Maga\u00f1a would inspect the dental work of other Mexican dentists \u2014occasionally calling the police in for backup."], [2, "It was very hard, you know. We seen the police and comes there. He says\u2014and Mariana says\u2014your work is not good. You have to give the money back to the lady."], [1, "Police in Los Algodones exist to protect the industry and to make tourists feel safe. There's an old promotional video for Molar City still up on YouTube. It's from 12 years ago, and it's less than a minute long. In the clip, a police officer does a salsa twirl with an elderly white woman. The video is titled \"All Tourists Are Safe and Fun in Los Lagodones.\"."]], "participants": []}, "input_categories": ["Society & Culture"]}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["medical tourism", "dental care", "mexico", "border towns", "patient safety", "healthcare standards", "clinic marketing", "reputation management", "cultural bias", "xenophobia", "americanization", "tourism industry", "policing", "customer complaints", "sanitation"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
