{"timestamp": 1778543040.4539433, "duration_sec": 1.7379875183105469, "validator_hotkey": "5HNe4Yx7idMcgrsoPogLVko2AA8Pap33aNyXQhpwBxsS4H4Q", "task_type": "conversation_tagging", "task_raw": {"mode": "mainnet", "api_version": 2.1, "guid": "HIDDEN", "bundle_guid": "HIDDEN", "type": "conversation_tagging", "scoring_mechanism": "ground_truth_tag_similarity_scoring", "input": {"guid": "HIDDEN", "input_type": "conversation", "data": {"window_idx": -1, "window": [[0, "clearly people forget more and more that there is a world outside of their sphere of interest. I feel more and more people somehow forget that there are people that think different from them that are not completely stupid. It's possible to think, to have different opinions than you have while also being fairly"], [0, "rational, but you simply make different trade offs on core values, let's say that I feel that gets forgotten more and more. So our question should be, can we find a way to ideally to monetize"], [0, "this engaging people with these different points of views because ultimately that's going to be the only way we can achieve it if there's a way to monetize this right some of these people will say that rationality and the enlightenment values and science are meta narratives and they don't really exist so it's quite difficult screw these it's rude it's quite difficult to talk these people are clearly people that think something else that i think that are clearly dumb"], [0, "Yeah, so I think what's interesting is that if you go into any detail about a topic and you're like actually willing to engage on having a dialogue and exploring the logic, on almost any topic you can find reasonable people on both sides. But where that often gets lost is that whether you have 144 characters or whatever it is, there's just no nuance and there's no room for like"], [0, "actually talking about what's underneath the surface level. And I think that has driven people to have these one answers for every question. It's free markets is the answer to everything or racism or systemic whatever is like causing all of society's problems. And it's like this dogmatic or ideologue type behavior of this very simple explanation for the world. But the world is not simple, super complicated. And maybe there's this adverse"], [0, "causal impact of life. We've been inundated with so much information that our feeble human mind is not capable of dealing with it, so it like reverts back to these very simple explanations. But whatever the case may be, somehow I think if you could bring nuance and long-form discussions back into it, then"], [0, "you can see like, all right, well, there's actually people that disagree with me, but they have valid points. And maybe at the end of the day, we still disagree, but I can at least recognize that they're not hateful bigots. We just happen to disagree and that's okay. And then we like figure out the right policy from there. But I don't know how to get that into Twitter, for instance."], [0, "Yeah, ain't no one got time for that, though, have they? It comes back to that psychological thing where we have this really short attention span. I even noticed myself doing this. I'll maybe open up Twitter, I'll scroll through a few things, and I'll find myself just scrolling, not really seeing much, and suddenly there'll be a picture or something that jumps out at me. It clearly triggers a neuron in my primitive brain that causes me to stop and look at it."], [0, "We're not built for having that detailed discussion unless we're really prepared for it. We're going to sit down, we're going to carve out a certain hour of the day to challenge ourselves mentally. And I guess most people just don't want to do that. They'd much rather turn on the Netflix and chill out after the end of a busy day. In a sense, you're confirming the findings of the film, which is that our attention span is decreasing."], [0, "And we need to hold our attention because it's exploration versus exploitation. Sometimes things need to get a little bit worse before they get better. We commit to a relationship through hard times and good times, but now we have Tinder. And now we have censorship by concision. If you can't fit it into 140 characters, then you can't learn anything. So what we're doing is we're making it very difficult for people to discover something new because it's so easy for them to find their own differential reality."]], "participants": []}}, "prompt_chain": [{"step": 0, "id": "12346546888", "crc": 1321321, "title": "Infer tags from a conversation window", "name": "infer_tags_from_a_conversation_window", "description": "Returns tags representing the conversation as a whole from the window received.", "type": "inference", "input_path": "conversation", "prompt_template": "Analyze conversation in terms of topic interests of the participants. Analyze the conversation (provided in structured XML format) where <p0> has the questions and <p1> has the answers. Return comma-delimited tags. Only return the tags without any English commentary.", "output_variable": "final_output", "output_type": "List[str]"}], "example_output": {"tags": ["guitar", "barn", "farm", "nashville"], "type": "List[str]"}}, "result": {"tags": ["social media", "attention span", "polarization", "political discourse", "dialogue", "nuance", "free speech", "censorship", "misinformation", "ideology", "critical thinking", "media consumption", "monetization", "psychology", "technology and society"], "vectors": null}, "error": null}
